\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 02 \'b7 The Problem\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 New designers didn't start on Raspberry AI. They started on a call. A scheduled session with a Raspberry rep walking them through the platform.\
That call wasn't just teaching tools. It answered a bigger question: where does this fit into how I already work? Raspberry is built as separate tools, but a designer's work runs in workflows. Turning sketches into product images with multiple colourways crosses several tools in sequence. Without that shape, the platform read as fragmented features with no obvious connection.\
Asset: 02-1.png; 02-2.png\
Raspberry raised the cost themselves, calling onboarding a significant drain on their team's time. They were also building Boards, a canvas workspace that worked nothing like the classic platform \'97 new to everyone, including existing designers.\
Two problems, in that order: replace a human onboarding path, then teach an interaction model the first solution wasn't built for.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 03 \'b7 Constraints\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 One tutorial experience, two interaction paradigms \'97 designed two months apart.
\f1\b0  Classic shipped first; Boards came later, so the second system had to extend a pattern built before I knew what it would need to cover.\

\f0\b Tutorials had to cross tool boundaries.
\f1\b0  Raspberry scoped by workflow, not by feature, so no tutorial could live inside a single tool. Every step had to anchor to screens someone else designed.\

\f0\b Two audiences starting from opposite places.
\f1\b0  New designers knew nothing. Existing ones knew Classic and had to unlearn it for Boards.\

\f0\b No access to product analytics while designing.
\f1\b0  I had no drop-off data, so no way to tell which step in a workflow was losing people.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 04 \'b7 The Decisions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf0 1 \'97 Teach the workflow, not the tool\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Asset: 04-1-1.png; 04-1-2.png\
The product is organized by tool but the work is organized by workflow.\
Per-tool tutorials were the cheaper answer, self-contained and easy to scope. A middle option existed too: per-tool tutorials and a map of how they connect. Workflow tutorials meant one tutorial running across several tools in sequence.\
I went with workflows because the objective was comprehension of fit. Per-tool tutorials teach mechanics but can't show where Raspberry sits in a designer's process. A map can label a seam but doesn\'92t walk anyone across it. Only a sequence does that, which is what the onboarding call had been doing.\
The cost: a tutorial could no longer belong to a single screen. Progress had to survive navigation between tools, and a designer who dropped out mid-workflow needed a way back to where they'd left off.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 2 \'97 Teach the canvas by walking through it, not by showing it\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 By the time Boards came back into scope, the classic tutorials had shipped. The question now was whether the existing pattern could stretch onto a canvas. Boards is an open canvas where a designer can do anything, in any order, or nothing at all.\
Asset: 04-2-1.png\
The first option was creating example files. A complete workflow laid out on a board, sketch through to recoloured product images, with notes around the canvas highlighting features and tips. A designer could duplicate it and use it as a playground.\
Asset: 04-2-2.png\
The second was to bring the classic app's event-based steps into Boards anyway, carrying over the notes from the first idea, walking a designer through the same sketch-to-recolour sequence, click by click, highlighting important points.\
I decided on the second: an example file shows a finished workflow, not how to get there, and that was the whole objective. A designer looking at a completed board sees what Raspberry can produce, not where it fits in their process.\
The first option was materially cheaper, and I knew that when I argued against it. Raspberry backed the call and spent the engineering time rather than ship something faster that only showed the destination.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

